WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:05.280
Violence, gore, and oh, so much nudity.&nbsp;
Plenty of ways to get an R-rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.080
but who knew all it takes to get on the MPAA’s&nbsp;
bad side is a little magic and an F-bomb or two?

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.780
Written and directed by John Hughes, the 1985 teen&nbsp;
drama "The Breakfast Club" stars five members of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:21.060
Hollywood's so-called "Brat Pack": Anthony Michael&nbsp;
Hall, Molly Ringwald, Emilio Estevez, Judd Nelson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.320
and Ally Sheedy. Set at a fictional high school,&nbsp;
the story revolves around a group of students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.860
from different walks of life who seemingly&nbsp;
share nothing in common other than the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.420
that they've all been assigned Saturday morning&nbsp;
detention. There we meet angry, malcontent Bender,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.760
morose recluse Allison, cocky jock Andrew,&nbsp;
awkward and nerdy Brian, and snobby Claire.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.240
Over the course of the film, the five&nbsp;
misfits muse over the struggles in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.360
vastly different lives, and they each come&nbsp;
to understand each other a little better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.860
It's a tender, heartfelt coming-of-age&nbsp;
story with few equals in its day. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:53.340
it earned itself an R-rating thanks to&nbsp;
its frank discussion of sex and drugs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.353
some crude language, plus a scene&nbsp;
where they get high together.

00:00:56.353 --> 00:01:00.720
"Chicks cannot hold their&nbsp;
smoke. That's what it is."

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.440
But while edgy for the '80s, "The Breakfast&nbsp;
Club" isn't actually all that explicit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.820
using suggestive euphemisms more often&nbsp;
than not. And while some mild drug use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.020
may have riled parents in 1985, it&nbsp;
hardly seems all that R-rated today.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.780
Written and directed by Cameron Crowe, "Almost&nbsp;
Famous" is inspired by the filmmaker's own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.580
unique life story, paralleling his days as a teen&nbsp;
journalist for Rolling Stone. The film follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.540
15-year-old William Miller, who worms his way&nbsp;
into the inner circle of an up-and-coming rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:32.220
group called Stillwater. Along with alluring&nbsp;
bohemian groupie Penny Lane, William joins the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.660
band on tour, going on a journey of self-discovery&nbsp;
as he learns about life, love, and friendship.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:39.480
A touching story of a young man&nbsp;
looking to find his own path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.599
"Almost Famous" got its R-rating thanks to&nbsp;
some of the usual swear words and drug use.

00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:45.000
"I'm on drugs!"

00:01:48.078 --> 00:01:52.380
What might have crossed the line, however, was&nbsp;
a single partial nude scene where Penny Lane is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.280
briefly seen dancing with her shirt hanging&nbsp;
open. But take that out and what you're left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.900
with is a rather subdued story — where most of&nbsp;
the sex and drugs are only implied — and with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.460
none of the raucous antics you might expect from&nbsp;
a more wild and crazy rock n' roll biopic. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:09.360
its poignant story of youthful rebellion&nbsp;
and the moral lessons it peddles, it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:12.840
almost a crime that its target audience&nbsp;
was kept out of theaters by its R-rating.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.440
An absolutely iconic buddy comedy, "Blazing&nbsp;
Saddles" is a 1974 Western classic starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.940
Gene Wilder and Cleavon Little as a pair of&nbsp;
mismatched partners in the Old West. Written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:28.680
and directed by Mel Brooks, it puts Little into&nbsp;
the role of Bart, a black laborer who becomes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.180
new sheriff of Rock Ridge. But Bart is only given&nbsp;
the job because the corrupt governor hopes it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:38.100
prompt the townsfolk to flee, allowing a railroad&nbsp;
to dismantle the town. When Bart gets wind of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:42.420
plan, though, he recruits local outlaw Jim to&nbsp;
help him stop the governor's dastardly scheme.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:47.700
A major hit, "Blazing Saddles" pulled in&nbsp;
a whopping $119 million at the box office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.500
thanks to the chemistry of its star pair and&nbsp;
a sidesplitting script from Brooks. A wildly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.400
successful movie among critics too, it's been&nbsp;
hailed as one of the best comedies of all time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.260
and even earned itself four stars from Roger&nbsp;
Ebert. But despite its success, we're forced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.520
wonder how much more popular it might have been&nbsp;
had it not received an R-rating, mostly because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.880
it doesn't deserve it. Sure, in 1974, some of the&nbsp;
more vulgar jokes may have offended uptight types.

00:03:12.059 --> 00:03:16.270
*fart sounds*

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.020
Looking back, though, it wouldn't earn that&nbsp;
rating today, as much of what crossed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:25.940
line at the time is more veiled humor&nbsp;
and innuendo than anything explicit.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.120
When it comes to action movies, it&nbsp;
can be fairly easy for a tame flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:34.740
to just barely cross the line and earn an&nbsp;
R-rating. In the case of "Constantine," we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.920
don't suspect it was the violence, blood,&nbsp;
or death that got it a stricter label,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.840
but its overall tone and dark themes. The film&nbsp;
centers on the eponymous John Constantine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.920
who has been sent to Hell, but is back on Earth&nbsp;
where he fights for righteousness — hunting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.820
demons and fallen angels — in an effort to&nbsp;
stave off his soul's delivery to Lucifer.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.620
The movie has some fantasy violence, and there&nbsp;
are a few grim moments, but the overall tone is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.820
often rather tongue-in-cheek. Nevertheless,&nbsp;
it's the demonic nature of the story that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.900
most likely to blame for its harsh rating.&nbsp;
The MPAA cautioned viewers that the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.800
contained "violence and demonic images,"&nbsp;
but the violence hardly seems any worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:12.780
than your standard PG-13 blockbuster. And while&nbsp;
hellish visuals may be cause for concern for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:16.599
more devout religious folk, it might not&nbsp;
even register as harsh for anyone else.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:21.480
It's certainly not a surprise to learn that&nbsp;
one of the most popular horror movies of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.700
2010s is rated R. But when you actually sit down&nbsp;
to watch "The Conjuring," the 2013 ghost story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:31.020
by director James Wan, you might be surprised&nbsp;
to find it's really not that shocking at all.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:36.240
The film tells the story of a pair of supernatural&nbsp;
experts played by Patrick Wilson and Vera Farmiga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.020
who are brought in to investigate a potential&nbsp;
haunting at a remote farmhouse. Given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.580
R-rating by the MPAA for "terror," it seems it&nbsp;
was the overall spooky vibe that rankled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.177
labeling agency rather than any explicit&nbsp;
bloodshed or even disturbing visuals.

00:04:49.735 --> 00:04:50.357
*clap clap*

00:04:53.305 --> 00:04:57.360
Because frankly, the film isn't really&nbsp;
all that violent. And according to Wan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.560
that was by design, as he'd held out hope of&nbsp;
the film making it out with a PG-13 rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.480
in an effort to attract a wider audience.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.340
But as the film's producer Walter Hamada told&nbsp;
fans during an appearance at WonderCon not long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:13.200
before its release, the MPAA had told him they&nbsp;
were stuck with a stricter rating. He explained:

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.900
"It's just so scary. [There&nbsp;
are] no specific scenes or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.880
tone you could take out to get it PG-13."

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.080
Eighth Grade

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:23.460
It seems like critically acclaimed coming-of-age&nbsp;
stories can often fall victim to the R-rating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.940
despite it not being warranted. That&nbsp;
might be because being earnest and true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:31.560
to life sometimes means showing young, often&nbsp;
underage characters acting crudely and vulgar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.820
or engaging in taboo activities like sex&nbsp;
and drugs as they navigate their way into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:39.900
adulthood. Such is the case with comedian&nbsp;
Bo Burnham's full-length directorial debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:44.640
"Eighth Grade," which introduces us to Kayla Day,&nbsp;
an ordinary middle schooler struggling with stress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.000
at school and at home as she prepares&nbsp;
to graduate the titular year of school.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.860
Aimed at an audience of teens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:53.340
the film is a tender and heartbreaking&nbsp;
look at adolescence and was praised by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.180
critics and young audiences for its realistic&nbsp;
depiction of growing up in modern America.

00:05:57.658 --> 00:06:00.540
"What was in there?"

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.880
"Just sort of my hopes and dreams."

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.900
Unfortunately, thanks to some pervasive&nbsp;
profanity and a couple of suggestive moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:10.680
the film garnered an R-rating, preventing&nbsp;
kids who might relate to it from seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:14.760
it in the theater on their own. Of course, this&nbsp;
effectively cut them out from seeing it at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:18.959
as we're not sure any 15-year-old wants to&nbsp;
be going to the movies with their parents.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:25.380
A watershed movie from 1999 that mixed sci-fi and&nbsp;
action, "The Matrix" revolutionized both genres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.100
with groundbreaking special effects and&nbsp;
a mind-bending, immersive story that had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:33.480
audiences questioning reality itself. Full of&nbsp;
dazzling visuals that included the innovative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.160
"bullet time" effect, the film's mix of rapid-fire&nbsp;
martial arts moves and break-neck, guns-blazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.840
shootouts had the audience's jaws on the floor.&nbsp;
The film follows Neo, a young hacker played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:47.520
by Keanu Reeves, who is recruited by Laurence&nbsp;
Fishburne's enigmatic Morpheus, and learns that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:51.900
the world as he knows it is merely a constructed&nbsp;
reality, and he is the key to saving mankind.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:57.180
Exploring deeply philosophical issues like fate&nbsp;
vs. free will and the nature of identity, "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.160
Matrix" captivated movie-goers like few films ever&nbsp;
had. But with plenty of violence, including loads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.360
of hot lead sprayed like water, and shoot-outs&nbsp;
that left dozens dead, this is one film that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:11.040
might not come as a surprise to learn was rated R.&nbsp;
But watch the film with a more discerning eye and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:15.000
you might start to rethink everything you thought&nbsp;
you knew about it. Because on closer inspection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.260
you'll notice that most of the violence is&nbsp;
almost entirely bloodless — and the victims all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:24.600
simulations. With a few minor tweaks, "The Matrix"&nbsp;
could probably have snagged a PG-13 rating.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:30.300
Spun out of a series of impromptu performances&nbsp;
from "Saturday Night Live," the 1980 film "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:34.320
Blues Brothers," is directed by John&nbsp;
Landis and stars John Belushi and Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:39.360
Aykroyd. They put on fedoras, suits, and shades to&nbsp;
become Jake and Elwood Blues, a pair of musically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.760
inclined ex-cons who find themselves clashing with&nbsp;
police, a gang of Neo-Nazis, a rival music group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:49.500
and a crazed killer, all while trying to save a&nbsp;
Catholic orphanage from going under. To complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.820
their "mission from God," the Blues Brothers&nbsp;
need to raise $5,000, which they plan to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.600
by putting on a musical showcase, and must&nbsp;
recruit other musicians to make it happen.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.140
With very little violence, "The Blues&nbsp;
Brothers" is much more like a classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:05.040
'80s caper and buddy comedy than any kind&nbsp;
of violent action movie you might expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.120
would warrant an R-rating. But indeed,&nbsp;
that's just the rating it received,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.000
owed almost entirely to its&nbsp;
generous use of swear words.

00:08:12.598 --> 00:08:15.692
"Christ Jake, take it easy man."
"That word."&nbsp;

00:08:15.692 --> 00:08:17.302
"Ow, s---!"
"Jesus Christ!"&nbsp;

00:08:17.302 --> 00:08:19.676
"S---!"
"God dammit!"

00:08:19.676 --> 00:08:23.340
And while that did necessitate the harder&nbsp;
rating, the movie was a big hit anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.000
and the censors seemed to get it right the&nbsp;
next time around because the belated sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:31.200
"Blues Brothers 2000," was&nbsp;
handed a PG-13 rating in 1998.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:36.000
"The Craft" was a pioneer in the&nbsp;
sub-genre of supernatural teen dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:41.220
led by a foursome of troubled young women imbued&nbsp;
with otherworldly powers. Starring Robin Tunney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:46.260
Fairuza Balk, Neve Campbell, and Rachel True,&nbsp;
the film's mix of coming-of-age story and dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:51.420
supernatural horror may have been a poor choice&nbsp;
if they were looking for a PG-13 rating. Combining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:55.560
those two elements may have been what doomed&nbsp;
it to the hard R-rating, much to the chagrin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.100
of younger teens — although the film did&nbsp;
still manage to be a hit at the box office.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:03.840
Admittedly, there are a few moments in "The Craft"&nbsp;
that could have rightfully warranted its label:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:07.800
There are a couple deaths that border on&nbsp;
graphic, as well as a scene where one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:11.400
the young women is the target of a failed&nbsp;
sexual assault. But it's not that far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:15.540
from most of the PG-13 thrillers you'd see&nbsp;
today. Sure, there's some crude language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:20.340
and vague discussion of sex and drugs, though&nbsp;
almost none of it is shown on screen. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.240
might have pushed it over the edge, however,&nbsp;
is a wrist-slashing scene that includes plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.080
of blood. It's a disturbing sequence that&nbsp;
some audiences might understandably want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.700
to be cautioned against. But considering that&nbsp;
the 2020 sequel, "The Craft: Legacy," wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:38.040
much different and garnered a PG-13, it makes one&nbsp;
wonder if the original's R was a bit of a reach.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:42.900
In what might be one of the most egregious&nbsp;
examples of a movie being given a harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:47.580
rating than it deserved, the 2010 drama "The&nbsp;
King's Speech" was slapped with an R for one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:51.780
simple reason: too many "F" words. But&nbsp;
the use of profanity wasn't gratuitous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:55.560
or unmotivated and was actually a key&nbsp;
component in the story of the life of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:59.520
England's King George VI, who suffered&nbsp;
from a debilitating speech impediment.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:04.500
Set in the 1930s, "The King's Speech" is the&nbsp;
remarkable true story of the real Prince Albert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.520
who is faced with the terrifying prospect of&nbsp;
giving a national speech on his ascension to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:12.900
King after his brother abdicates the throne.&nbsp;
The crux of the story centers on his treatment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:17.340
for a stammer with the help of speech therapist,&nbsp;
Lionel Logue. Logue is able to help Albert with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:21.480
some unorthodox methods and eventually makes&nbsp;
the realization that swearing can help abate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:25.380
Albert's stutter. And it's a now-famous&nbsp;
scene that led to the film's R-rating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:29.651
as Albert lets loose a torrent of comical&nbsp;
obscenities in a session with Logue.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:33.009
"Bugger bugger buggity buggity&nbsp;
buggity f--- f--- ass!"&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:33.620
"Yes!"

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:38.400
Unfortunately, because of its importance to&nbsp;
the story, the swearing is not a superficial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:42.000
element that can be easily excised to&nbsp;
get a lighter rating. But no matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.420
because the film went on to win critical&nbsp;
acclaim en route to capturing the Academy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:49.860
Award for best picture, and earning its&nbsp;
lead, Colin Firth, an Oscar for best actor.

